// @trap
// @hint redundant type annotation: `ff64` is inferred as `f64`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `frac` is inferred as `f64`
// THE FAILURE MODE THE RULING ADDED. `as-cast-trap-nan.vl` and `as-cast-trap-oob.vl` pin the
// two that always trapped — the trapping `trunc` family refuses a NaN and an out-of-range
// value on its own. A FRACTION did not: `3.9 as! i32` truncated to `3` and said nothing,
// which is the D1041 defect (the `!` was accepted and dropped).
//
// Under DECISIONS.md §"Numeric `as` to an INTEGER target is exact-or-fail under the trio" a
// fraction is a failure like any other, so `as!` aborts — and the abort carries a
// SOURCE-LOCATED reason first (`as! i32 at <line>:<col>: not exact`), which is the diagnostic
// half of the old `f64-as-i32-out-of-range` ask. The trailing print never runs.
//
// The spelled loss is `trunc(frac) as! i32`, which is `as-cast-exact-trio.vl`'s peephole row.
function ff64(x: f64): f64 { x }
const frac: f64 = ff64(3.9)
print(frac as! i32)
print(-1)
